Brute-forcing in JavaScript typically refers to the process of systematically trying all possible combinations of characters to guess a password or to find a solution to a problem. It's important to note that brute-forcing is generally considered unethical and illegal when used to gain unauthorized access to systems or data. However, understanding the concept can be useful for learning about security and for implementing protective measures.

Here's a simple example of how you might implement a very basic brute-force algorithm in JavaScript to guess a 4-digit PIN. This is for educational purposes only:

```javascript
function bruteForcePin(pin) {
  const digits = '0123456789';
  for (let i = 0; i < digits.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < digits.length; j++) {
      for (let k = 0; k < digits.length; k++) {
        for (let l = 0; l < digits.length; l++) {
          const guess = digits[i] + digits[j] + digits[k] + digits[l];
          if (guess === pin) {
            console.log(`Found the PIN: ${guess}`);
            return;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  console.log('PIN not found.');
}

// Example usage:
const targetPin = '1234'; // The PIN you want to guess
bruteForcePin(targetPin);
```

This code will try every combination of the digits 0-9 for a 4-digit PIN. When it finds the correct PIN, it will print it to the console.

Remember, this is a very basic example and real-world applications would require much more sophisticated and secure methods. Additionally, brute-forcing can be very time-consuming and is not a recommended approach for legitimate purposes. Always use proper authentication methods and respect privacy and security laws.

