#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    std::vector<int> results;

    // Lambda function to run on each element
    auto process = [](int x) { return x * x; };

    // Use std::transform to apply the lambda to each element
    std::transform(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), std::back_inserter(results), process);

    // Print the results
    for (int result : results) {
        std::cout << result << " ";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the lambda function `process` is defined to square a number. The `std::transform` function applies this lambda to each element in the `numbers` vector, storing the results in the `results` vector. This approach allows you to run the same code inline on multiple named variables (in this case, the elements of the `numbers` vector) efficiently.

